00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:20.359 align:start position:0%
 
welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Genevieve LeBaron a senior lecturer  in politics at the University of  Sheffield in the UK and human  trafficking and modern day slavery  fellow at the Gilder Lehrman Center for  the Study of slavery resistance and  abolition at the Macmillan Center she's  the author of protest Inc the  corporatization of activism with Peter  Davern as well as numerous journal  articles she also writes widely for the  media and is founder and editor of the  beyond trafficking and slavery section  of open democracy net which has over  nine million readers last year she was  awarded the British Academy for the  humanities and Social Sciences  rising star engagement award today we'll  talk with her about global labor  standards and exploitation  welcome professor LeBaron thank you for  having very nice to have you here with  us today so let's start with a kind of  an overview of the book project you're  working on tell us about it  sure the book project that I'm working  on here in my fellowship at Yale is  looking at what the big brand companies  are doing about forced labor human  trafficking and slavery in their supply  chains so that's looking at companies  like Nike Walmart Apple Nestle who in  the past 15 years or so have been  increasingly concerned about the issues  of forced labor and trafficking in their  supply chains around the world the  International Labor Organization  estimates about 21 million victims of  forced labor in the world today and most  of that forced labor does take place in  the private economy so in the supply  chains of companies like those that I  mentioned and so over the past 15 years  or so those companies have launched a  whole bunch of initiatives ranging from  different types of auditing to data  analytics  to multi-stakeholder initiatives  certifications like Fairtrade just a  whole spate of activity to try to  address the issue of forced labour and  their supply chain and what I'm looking  at in the book is the effectiveness of  those types of initiatives okay are they  actually working to trigger change on  the ground to actually address you know  the prevalence of forced labour within  these chains or are they in some ways  potentially driving the problem deeper  into the shadows of the global economy  and so I'm looking at this question of  effectiveness I'm looking at the  question of how are the private  initiatives that companies are launching  interacting with the public ones that  states are taking do things like  regulation labor inspection are they  complementing each other are the  corporate initiatives supplementing and  strengthening the public regulatory  initiatives or is there a trade-off  going on where the private ones are  taking a sort of greater role in the  governance fear and the public ones are  becoming less rigorous and potentially  have less teeth so those are the types  of things I'm getting at okay  and what led you to be interested in  this obviously difficult topic yeah  that's a good question in some ways it  goes back quite a ways for me when I was  an undergraduate in Washington State  there was a real problem going on with  prison labor so that was in the late you  know early 2000s there was a lot of  companies using prison labor as a  alternative to sort of outsourcing  overseas so there were reports of  companies like Boeing Starbucks Nike  Victoria's Secret usually through you  know their suppliers or subsidiaries but  setting up shop in prisons and and using  prisoners as a labor force instead of  you know free workers on the outside and  so I became interested at that time in  the community that I was living there  was a lot of concern about this and  there was you know the question of is  does this violate prisoners Human Rights  you have people who are  usually in prison in the US for poverty  related crimes unable to find jobs in  many cases then being made to work in  prison usually with very little pay and  without a real ability to to refuse that  work and so I became interested in that  less than the human rights elements of  it and more in the kind of political  economic role that it was playing in the  US economy what were companies getting  out of this in terms of it but mostly  what was the government getting out of  it you know what what was the government  gaining by opening up their prisons to  industry and so I just became interested  in that relationship between private  companies and the private economy and  forms of labor that are non-standard  that we tend to think of as being more  antiquated prison labor of course goes  very very far back in the history of US  capitalism and emerged in the sort of  aftermath of slavery and it was an  exception to the abolition of slavery so  it's a form of involuntary servitude so  my interest really goes back to that  that point of these things that we tend  to think of as antiquated relations why  do they emerge why are they so resilient  in the global economy and why haven't  the forces of modernity and the kind of  modern corporation eradicated these  forms of forced labor and why did some  companies seem to make use of them as an  alternative to a public labor force okay  I think many people would be somewhat  surprised to know that modern-day  slavery is happening as a part of  corporate America so I'm I'm imagining  that this is going on in countries where  corporations do business when they farm  at work overseas  so can you let's talk specifics give us  a couple of examples of things that you  know about that are currently going on  sure yeah I mean forced labor takes a  number of different forms it has been  documented in almost every country in  the world  so it's not exclusively a problem that  that's confined to the global South  though as you're saying a lot of it does  take place in in the in the global south  just to give a few examples of what this  kind of looks like The Guardian is a  uk-based newspaper recently did a  six-month undercover investigation of  the shrimp industry in Thailand which  was a real shock I think to a lot of  Americans because that investigation  uncovered that forced labor human  trafficking slavery were rampant in an  industry that supplies to the big  retailers you know in the u.s. in the UK  and Europe like Costco and Tesco and  what was happening there was that people  were being recruited usually migrant  workers from the neighboring countries  like Burma into the Thai fishing  industry and the workers were paying the  recruiters these third-party agents a  fee to find them a job in in the Thai  shrimp industry and so they were paying  this fee and then they were essentially  being sold off to boat captains where  they were at sea they were in you know  you know kind of hidden parts of the  economy they're there at sea there's not  a lot of surveillance no one's really  checking and there were rampant abuses  that ranged from torture to manipulation  of debt and wages all sorts of practices  that were classified as slavery so  that's one way that this can manifest  another type of example in you know in a  more manufacturing industry would be  Apple has recently taken a really  important step in banning what they  called involuntary servitude and their  supply chain so what they discovered  over the past year so was that in some  of their supplier factories like Foxconn  Pegatron in China a similar dynamic was  happening with recruiters going to find  workers who really needed work workers  who were pretty vulnerable often  migrants who didn't have any real  opportunities to work where they were  living  and so then they'd pay this the the  workers would pay a fee to the recruiter  they'd end up in the supplier factory  already very much in debt so the fee  might be a month's wages and then that  debt was used to impose very difficult  working conditions sometimes involuntary  overtime a lot of the dynamics we  associate with forced labor and so what  Apple did to their credit I think is  that they said you know workers can't be  made to pay those fees actually the  supplier firm needs to pay those fees to  the recruiter and even though ultimately  that meant Apple itself will be you know  having to cost that into their business  model they were willing to do that so  they've refunded four million dollars  recently to workers but a lot of  companies you know that's a practice  that happens pretty endemically within a  lot of industries and very few companies  have taken that kind of leading role  that Apple has taken to address it in  the way that they have okay in in  working on the book how are you doing  the research for it I would imagine in a  variety of ways are you talking to  actually actual people in companies  where things like this happen yeah  that's a great question firstly for as  you can well imagine is an extremely  difficult topic to research it's illegal  in almost every country in the world and  for good reasons you know companies and  governments are very hesitant to give  researchers access to their workers and  supply chains so one of the things I've  been working on over the past couple of  years is an initiative with the British  Academy looking at methods for forced  labor research so that's been bringing  together a number of us who are working  on social scientific research on forced  labor to talk about how we can  strengthen the methods at the moment  there's no country level data on forced  labor that's reliable there's there's  some very good case studies of forced  labor by sector so that have looked at  forced labor in the electronics industry  or in the garment industry or in a  particular country and what I've done in  my book is use that sector level data as  a sort of starting point to  understanding the dye  Amyx and the patterns of how some of  this is actually playing out but what  I've done in my book is I've I have  about three different strategies of  research that I've been carrying out  over the past few years so one is that  I've looked at sector level data on  forced labor I've done some studies of  forced labor in the UK in the cannabis  food and construction industries and  that's given me a really good sense of  the patterns of how these practices  manifest but also some of what companies  are doing to address them I've also  taken a look at audit firm data so  that's the data that audit firms are  collecting they're the ones who are kind  of going in and being the boots on the  ground in terms of conducting these  inspections of factories mining sites  the like and so they have a lot of  knowledge of how these practices have  evolved over time whether we're seeing  increases or decreases in different  sectors etc so I've used some of that to  gain insights about whether you know how  how practices are evolving and the last  thing that I've done is I've interviewed  a lot of people with first-hand  knowledge of the corporate social  responsibility initiatives and their  effectiveness  so that ranges from industry  representatives to auditors to people  involved with multi-stakeholder  initiatives really just a range of  different people I've conducted about a  hundred interviews on these questions to  try to get the best knowledge that's  available about whether these  initiatives are effective under what  circumstances they're effective and what  the gaps are in terms of further work  that needs to be done  okay so what what are the gaps in terms  of laws that are on the books at this  point I mean how are these people  falling through the cracks so to speak  mmm-hmm  I mean that that's a very good question  I think what we're seeing you mentioned  law in the books I think one of the  things we're seeing is that there's  actually a very good laws on the books  of a lot of countries and that the  problem is coming  one of the main problems is coming in  terms of enforcement of those laws so in  a lot of countries labor inspectorate's  have been cut the budget for labor  inspection has been reduced and so you  have real challenges in terms of  governments actually monitoring and  enforcing their own laws and in the wake  of that private the private sector has  really taken on a greater role but what  we're seeing is that some of those  initiatives like auditing don't  necessarily work as well as the public  sector initiatives to inspect factories  and so there are a number of challenges  that come up in relation to the private  sector having really taken on board the  issue of forced labor trafficking and  slavery but not necessarily pursuing the  types of initiatives and policies that  that seem to address the root causes of  these problems on the ground so what  what do you suggest to help fix the  problem  what do you think would work I think  that that's a very big yes I know and  I'm I know I'm putting you on the spot  but you know what are your ideas yeah I  think that what we need to start looking  at is we need to start looking closely  at the root causes of the problems  within business models so there's been a  real attention to the symptoms of the  problems of forced labor addressing you  know the worst exploitation Xand really  you know trying to break those people  out of slavery type situations and  that's been really important work that  NGOs and others have conducted but what  we need to start looking much more  closely at are the root causes of why  these types of exploitation are thriving  in the first place and that gets into  questions around things like  subcontracting the relationship between  the big brands and the suppliers and  their supply chain it gets at questions  around the price of goods you know  there's been a lot of pressure going on  to produce goods more and more cheaply  and sell them more and more cheaply and  that can create some real risks around  labor standards because it's very  difficult to produce goods  quickly cheaply on-time deliver them to  the retailer and do it in a way that  honors good labor practice all right so  you've seen on the one hand a rising  demand for sub minimum wage labor which  can sometimes take the form of forced  labor and supply chains and on the other  hand a burgeoning supply of people who  don't have decent work who are entering  into very vulnerable risky types of jobs  so you need to be able to combat both of  those sides of the root causes the  supply and the demand issues around  forced labor and supply chains okay  ultimately you know what are you hoping  people will take away from your book  that's another good question I guess I'm  hoping that people will take take away  the idea that these are systemic issues  there's been a lot of attention on you  know different sectors so the  effectiveness say of Fairtrade on coffee  growers in Guatemala or the these types  of you know can we really look at  effectiveness on this micro scale and  combat these issues on a micro scale and  part of what I think I'm trying to get  at in in the book and trying to  understand in the book is whether that's  effective or whether we need to be  taking a much more systemic look at  these problems as problems that are  fundamentally rooted in how the global  economy itself is set up and organized  and run and governed and so I think that  reframing forced labor and trafficking  and slavery as global economic problems  that are really deeply rooted in the  broader structures of the global economy  things like inequality things like the  power of companies via via governments  etc is what I'm really hoping to to to  understand and to raise questions about  in the book okay very good thank you so  much for being here with us today and  sharing some of your work thank you for  having me for more information about  Professor LeBaron and her research  please visit our website at macmillan  report yale.edu  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale

